\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Q1 (HKDSE 2023 Ch4.2 MC):} The figure shows a circuit with three identical light bulbs B1, B2 and B3 connected to a battery. In 1 s, B1, B2 and B3 dissipate energy of 4 J, 8 J and 4 J respectively. Which relationships are correct?

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
    \item Current through B3 : current through B2 = 1 : 2
    \item Voltage across B1 : voltage across B2 = 1 : 2
    \item Energy supplied by the battery : energy dissipated by B2 = 2 : 1
\end{enumerate}

A. (1) and (2) only \quad B. (1) and (3) only \quad C. (2) and (3) only \quad D. (1), (2) and (3)

\bigskip
\begin{center}
\fbox{\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{sample_page_trimmed-000.jpg}}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\textbf{Let's solve it — and in doing so, discover what current, voltage and energy are.}

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 1 — Read the circuit topology from the diagram.}

The diagram shows B1 and B2 in series with each other. That series combination is \emph{in parallel} with B3. This is the key — we cannot assume all three are in series.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 2 — Energy per second = Power.}

Since all three bulbs dissipate energy in 1 s:
$$P_1 = 4\text{ W},\quad P_2 = 8\text{ W},\quad P_3 = 4\text{ W}$$

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 3 — Find the resistance of each bulb using $P = V^2/R$.}

In parallel, the same voltage $V$ appears across each branch:
$$R_1 = \frac{V^2}{4},\qquad R_2 = \frac{V^2}{8},\qquad R_3 = \frac{V^2}{4}$$

Since B1 and B2 are in series, their combined resistance is:
$$R_{12} = R_1 + R_2 = \frac{V^2}{4} + \frac{V^2}{8} = \frac{3V^2}{8}$$

For the parallel combination, $R_{12}$ must equal $R_3$ (same voltage, same branch current):
$$\frac{3V^2}{8} = \frac{V^2}{4} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{this checks out — both branches have equal resistance.}$$

So $R_1 = R_3$, and $R_2 = \frac{1}{2}R_1$.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 4 — Check statement (1): current ratio.}

The two parallel branches have equal resistance, so the current splits equally:
$$I_{\text{series branch}} = I_{\text{parallel branch}}$$

Within the series branch, $I_1 = I_2$. So $I_3 : I_2 = 1 : 2$. \textbf{Statement (1) is TRUE.}

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 5 — Check statement (2): voltage ratio in series.}

B1 and B2 are in series, so $V \propto R$. Since $R_1 = 2 \times R_2$:
$$V_1 : V_2 = R_1 : R_2 = 2 : 1 \quad\Rightarrow\quad V_1 : V_2 = 1 : 2 \quad\checkmark$$

\textbf{Statement (2) is TRUE.}

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 6 — Check statement (3): energy ratio.}

Total power supplied by battery = $P_1 + P_2 + P_3 = 4 + 8 + 4 = 16$ W.

Energy supplied by battery in 1 s = 16 J.
Energy dissipated by B2 in 1 s = 8 J.

Energy ratio $= 16 : 8 = 2 : 1$. \textbf{Statement (3) is TRUE.}

\bigskip
\textbf{Answer: D — statements (1), (2) and (3) are all correct.}

\bigskip
\textbf{What did we just learn?} Three new words appeared: current ($I$), voltage ($V$) and power ($P$). The key insight is \textbf{reading the circuit topology} from the diagram — B1 and B2 are in series with each other, but that series combination is in \emph{parallel} with B3. This gives us our first mental model.
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Q17 (HKDSE Ch2 MC):} The figure shows a ``hill diagram'' of part of a circuit. What does the height of the diagram represent?

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
    \item Potential energy of electrons
    \item Kinetic energy of electrons
    \item Potential energy of positive charges
    \item Kinetic energy of positive charges
\end{enumerate}

\bigskip
\begin{center}
\fbox{\includegraphics[width=0.65\linewidth]{sample_page_trimmed-007.jpg}}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\textbf{Solution}

The hill diagram explicitly labels the height difference as $\Delta E = qV$, and $q = -e$ for an electron. This tells us directly that the height represents the \textbf{energy change of an electron} as it moves between the two potentials.

\begin{itemize}
    \item The diagram shows an electron (charge $q = -e$) moving through a resistor, losing energy.
    \item The height drop = $\Delta E = qV = (-e)V$, which is negative — meaning the electron loses potential energy.
    \item The diagram is labelled ``$\Delta E = qV$'' and ``$q = -e$ for an electron'' — these annotations make it unambiguous that the height represents the electron's potential energy.
    \item Positive charges would have $q = +e$ and would gain energy going from low to high potential, which is the opposite direction to the electron flow shown.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\textbf{Answer: (a)} Potential energy of electrons (i.e. the energy change $\Delta E = qV$ for an electron).
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Q36 (HKDSE Ch2 MC):} In the circuit below, what is the reading of the voltmeter when both switches S1 and S2 are open?

\bigskip
\begin{center}
\fbox{\includegraphics[width=0.60\linewidth]{sample_page_trimmed-013.jpg}}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\textbf{Solution}

When both switches are open, no current flows through the circuit (open circuit = infinite resistance).

The voltmeter is connected across the battery. With no current flowing, there is no voltage drop across any internal resistance of the battery, so the voltmeter measures the full battery EMF.

\bigskip
\textbf{Answer: 6 V.}
\end{exercise}

\bigskip

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Q37 (HKDSE Ch2 MC):} In the circuit shown, what is the reading of the voltmeter when both switches S1 and S2 are closed?

\bigskip
\begin{center}
\fbox{\includegraphics[width=0.60\linewidth]{sample_page_trimmed-014.jpg}}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\textbf{Solution}

When both switches are closed, they act as wires (0 $\Omega$ resistance).

Looking at the diagram: S2 is closed, connecting the two terminals of the voltmeter together through a wire. The voltmeter is connected directly across the wire — not across any component with a potential difference.

With S2 closed, the circuit is shorted at the voltmeter terminals, so the voltage between those two points is 0 V.

\bigskip
\textbf{Answer: 0 V.}
\end{exercise}

\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Q3 (HKDSE Ch2 MC):} Four identical resistors and a battery are connected as shown. An electron has the greatest energy change when it moves through which component?

\bigskip
\begin{center}
\fbox{\includegraphics[width=0.65\linewidth]{sample_page_trimmed-006.jpg}}
\end{center}

\bigskip
\textbf{Solution}

Energy change for a charge $q$ moving across a component with voltage $V$ is:
$$\Delta E = qV$$

Since the charge (electron, $q = -e$) is the same for all components, the greatest energy change occurs across the component with the greatest voltage drop.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 1 — Determine the circuit topology from the diagram.}

The diagram shows four identical resistors in a two-branch parallel arrangement: each branch has two resistors in series, and the two branches are in parallel with each other.

Since each branch has the same total resistance ($R + R = 2R$), the current splits equally between the two branches. Each branch carries half the total current.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 2 — Voltage across each resistor.}

In each branch, the two resistors in series share the branch voltage. Since both resistors are identical, the voltage drop across each individual resistor is half the branch voltage.

Because the two branches are identical, every resistor in the circuit has the same voltage drop.

\bigskip
\textbf{Step 3 — Conclusion.}

Since all four resistors are identical and see the same voltage drop, the electron loses the same amount of energy going through \emph{any} of the four resistors.

\bigskip
\textbf{Answer: Any of the four identical resistors — the energy change is the same for all of them.}
\end{exercise}

